1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,200
[The Sun fills the frame, alive and roaring, inside a faint lattice of thin bright slats. The slats turn, a wave rolling down from the north. The light strobes through the closing gaps. Black.]

2
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,635
NARRATOR: At six forty-four this morning, something closed around the Sun.

3
00:00:05,305 --> 00:00:08,455
[The last shell of sunlight races out toward a tiny blue Earth. The dark follows it.]

4
00:00:05,405 --> 00:00:08,420
NARRATOR: Its last light is still racing toward you.

5
00:00:08,790 --> 00:00:11,940
[Ride along the light front. Earth grows from a dot.]

6
00:00:08,890 --> 00:00:12,125
NARRATOR: It will reach Earth in eight minutes and twenty seconds.

7
00:00:12,495 --> 00:00:15,645
[A sunlit kitchen. You yawn over a mug. Mira draws at the table. Biscuit sits very politely beside the toast.]

8
00:00:12,595 --> 00:00:16,080
NARRATOR: So for eight more minutes, it is just a summer morning.

9
00:00:16,450 --> 00:00:19,600
[Close on Mira finishing a crayon sun. She holds it up, proud.]

10
00:00:16,550 --> 00:00:20,115
NARRATOR: Your little sister Mira is drawing the Sun. Again.

11
00:00:20,485 --> 00:00:22,940
[Close on Biscuit. His eyes slide slowly toward the toast on the edge of the table.]

12
00:00:20,585 --> 00:00:22,720
NARRATOR: Biscuit is planning a crime.

13
00:00:23,090 --> 00:00:26,240
[A bright beach. A surfer stands up on a perfect wave.]

14
00:00:23,190 --> 00:00:26,650
NARRATOR: Somewhere, someone catches the best wave of their life.

15
00:00:27,020 --> 00:00:29,805
[Golden rice terraces. A farmer straightens up and squints at the sky.]

16
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:29,585
NARRATOR: A farmer checks the sky for rain.

17
00:00:29,955 --> 00:00:33,105
[A kid sprints for a school bus. The doors close on his nose. The bus leaves.]

18
00:00:30,055 --> 00:00:32,890
NARRATOR: A kid misses the bus by one second.

19
00:00:33,260 --> 00:00:36,315
[The trailing edge of the light sweeps past the Moon and onto the day side of Earth.]

20
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:36,095
NARRATOR: And right behind the light comes the dark.

21
00:00:36,465 --> 00:00:38,325
[Biscuit rises on his back legs and snatches the toast. You point. Three. Two.]

22
00:00:36,565 --> 00:00:38,105
NARRATOR: Three. Two.

23
00:00:38,475 --> 00:00:40,725
[Mid-snatch, the sunlight snaps off. Everyone freezes in the blue dark. Biscuit still has the toast.]

24
00:00:38,575 --> 00:00:39,590
NARRATOR: One.

25
00:00:40,875 --> 00:00:43,105
[Through the kitchen window: stars over a city at breakfast time. Streetlights flicker on.]

26
00:00:40,975 --> 00:00:42,285
MIRA: Why is it night?

27
00:00:43,255 --> 00:00:46,305
[Title: THE SUN WENT OUT.]

28
00:00:46,455 --> 00:00:49,485
[Outside, the street is full. Pyjamas and work clothes, every phone held up to a starry sky.]

29
00:00:46,555 --> 00:00:49,265
NARRATOR: Every phone on the street is pointing up.

30
00:00:49,635 --> 00:00:52,390
[A man shouts at the sky, pointing.]

31
00:00:49,735 --> 00:00:52,170
NARRATOR: Someone shouts that it is an eclipse.

32
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:55,520
[A phone screen: NO SERVICE. The bars drop to zero.]

33
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:55,300
NARRATOR: Every network jams in the same minute.

34
00:00:55,670 --> 00:00:58,820
[Low over the rooftops, a thin crescent Moon is a black sliver cutting a hole in the stars.]

35
00:00:55,770 --> 00:00:59,860
NARRATOR: But eclipses last minutes. And the Moon has gone dark too.

36
00:01:00,230 --> 00:01:03,380
[Closer: the dead Moon, a faint grey ghost against the stars.]

37
00:01:00,330 --> 00:01:03,390
NARRATOR: Its light was only ever borrowed from the Sun.

38
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:06,595
[Back inside. Hard knocks rattle the door.]

39
00:01:03,860 --> 00:01:06,375
NARRATOR: Then someone hammers on your door.

40
00:01:06,745 --> 00:01:09,155
[The door opens on Mr. Ito from upstairs: cardigan, slippers, torch, a telescope case on his back.]

41
00:01:06,845 --> 00:01:08,935
MR. ITO: You. Come to the roof.

42
00:01:09,305 --> 00:01:12,240
[Ito points past you at Biscuit, who still has the toast.]

43
00:01:09,405 --> 00:01:12,020
MR. ITO: Bring the girl. And the dog.

44
00:01:12,390 --> 00:01:15,540
[The roof. Mr. Ito sets up a brass telescope with the joy of a child at Christmas.]

45
00:01:12,490 --> 00:01:18,055
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito taught astronomy for forty years. He has never been this excited.

46
00:01:18,425 --> 00:01:21,575
[Low in the east, one bright point still shining: Venus.]

47
00:01:18,525 --> 00:01:21,465
MR. ITO: Look. Venus is still shining.

48
00:01:21,835 --> 00:01:23,795
[Mira, outraged at the universe.]

49
00:01:21,935 --> 00:01:23,575
MIRA: But the Sun is gone!

50
00:01:23,945 --> 00:01:26,580
[Diagram: Venus as a mirror, sunlight bouncing off it toward Earth.]

51
00:01:24,045 --> 00:01:26,360
MR. ITO: Venus is a mirror.

52
00:01:26,730 --> 00:01:29,880
[The light path traced: Sun to Venus, Venus to Earth. Old light still in flight.]

53
00:01:26,830 --> 00:01:30,695
MR. ITO: The light it shows us left the Sun minutes ago.

54
00:01:31,065 --> 00:01:34,215
[Venus flickers and goes out.]

55
00:01:31,165 --> 00:01:33,950
NARRATOR: Three minutes later, Venus goes out.

56
00:01:34,820 --> 00:01:37,970
[Ito, lit by the torch, works it out.]

57
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:40,285
MR. ITO: If something blocked the Sun close to us, everything would go dark at once.

58
00:01:40,655 --> 00:01:43,690
[Ito turns to you, eyes huge behind his glasses.]

59
00:01:40,755 --> 00:01:43,170
MR. ITO: This happened at the Sun.

60
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:46,990
[Diagram: Jupiter, far out. A long light path. A clock.]

61
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:49,380
MR. ITO: Jupiter is much further out. It should go dark in seventy-two minutes.

62
00:01:49,750 --> 00:01:52,600
[Time-lapse: stars wheel overhead. Mira falls asleep against Biscuit (the toast is finally gone). You keep checking your watch.]

63
00:01:49,850 --> 00:01:52,135
NARRATOR: So you sit down, and you wait.

64
00:01:52,750 --> 00:01:55,900
[Jupiter, centred in the telescope crosshairs, winks out.]

65
00:01:52,850 --> 00:01:56,315
NARRATOR: Seventy-two minutes later, to the second, it does.

66
00:01:57,085 --> 00:01:59,135
[Ito does a small, silent victory dance. Biscuit joins in. Mira sleeps through it.]

67
00:01:59,285 --> 00:02:01,420
[Close on your watch: the thermometer ticks down a tenth of a degree.]

68
00:01:59,385 --> 00:02:01,200
NARRATOR: Then you look at your watch.

69
00:02:01,570 --> 00:02:04,720
[Earth from space, wrapped in night, shedding faint red heat into the black.]

70
00:02:01,670 --> 00:02:05,080
NARRATOR: Without the Sun, Earth is losing heat to space.

71
00:02:05,450 --> 00:02:07,760
[A thermometer on the balcony rail. Its red line sinks.]

72
00:02:05,550 --> 00:02:07,540
NARRATOR: A little more every hour.

73
00:02:07,910 --> 00:02:11,060
[The kitchen calendar. Its pages flip by themselves, month after month, to one date circled in red.]

74
00:02:08,010 --> 00:02:13,320
NARRATOR: By the end of this year, you'll know exactly what happened to the Sun. You won't like it.

75
00:02:14,090 --> 00:02:17,240
[Day 1. The whole street runs the same way: toward the supermarket.]

76
00:02:14,190 --> 00:02:17,455
NARRATOR: Day one. The whole city has the same idea.

77
00:02:17,825 --> 00:02:20,885
[Half-empty shelves. You steer a trolley and sweep things in as you name them.]

78
00:02:17,925 --> 00:02:20,665
NARRATOR: Candles. Rice. Batteries. Water.

79
00:02:21,035 --> 00:02:23,245
[Mira marches back with an armful of dog treats. Biscuit loses his mind.]

80
00:02:21,135 --> 00:02:23,025
NARRATOR: Mira has her own list.

81
00:02:23,395 --> 00:02:26,450
[Two grown men in a tug-of-war over the last electric heater.]

82
00:02:23,495 --> 00:02:26,230
NARRATOR: The last heater in the shop starts a fight.

83
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:29,750
[Home. You tape blankets over the windows of one room. Mira hands up the tape.]

84
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:29,560
NARRATOR: Back home, you pick one room and seal it.

85
00:02:29,930 --> 00:02:33,080
[Ito shuffles in with his duvet, a kettle and a framed photo of his late wife.]

86
00:02:30,030 --> 00:02:33,515
NARRATOR: Mr. Ito moves in with you. He lives alone.

87
00:02:33,885 --> 00:02:37,035
[Thermal-style diagram: each of you glows like a light bulb. Biscuit is a smaller bulb.]

88
00:02:33,985 --> 00:02:37,770
NARRATOR: Every person gives off about a hundred watts of heat.

89
00:02:38,140 --> 00:02:41,290
[All four squeezed together under blankets, glowing faintly orange.]

90
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:41,730
NARRATOR: Congratulations. You are all radiators now.

91
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:44,385
[You turn the bath tap on full. Water thunders in.]

92
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:44,165
NARRATOR: Then you fill the bathtub.

93
00:02:44,535 --> 00:02:47,685
[Close on a water pipe in the wall, frost creeping along it.]

94
00:02:44,635 --> 00:02:48,350
NARRATOR: When the pipes freeze, this is all the water you will have.

95
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:51,870
[You carry the food from the dead freezer out onto the frosted balcony.]

96
00:02:48,820 --> 00:02:53,055
NARRATOR: You will not need the freezer. The whole planet is becoming one.

97
00:02:53,425 --> 00:02:56,575
[A battery radio on the windowsill, its dial glowing.]

98
00:02:53,525 --> 00:02:58,665
(RADIO): Harbour officials say this morning’s high tide was completely normal.

99
00:02:59,035 --> 00:03:02,070
[Ito nearly jumps out of his slippers.]

100
00:02:59,135 --> 00:03:01,850
MR. ITO: Normal! The tide was normal!

101
00:03:02,220 --> 00:03:04,070
[Mira, deadpan.]

102
00:03:02,320 --> 00:03:03,160
MIRA: So?

103
00:03:04,220 --> 00:03:07,370
[Diagram: the Moon tugs a bulge of ocean toward it.]

104
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:07,860
MR. ITO: The Moon pulls the sea. So does the Sun.

105
00:03:08,230 --> 00:03:11,380
[The Sun’s bulge is removed: the tide shrinks by a third.]

106
00:03:08,330 --> 00:03:13,120
MR. ITO: Without the Sun, this week’s big tides would be a third smaller.

107
00:03:13,490 --> 00:03:16,640
[Ito takes off his door key and swings it round on its string.]

108
00:03:13,590 --> 00:03:17,080
MR. ITO: Watch. The string keeps the key going round.

109
00:03:17,450 --> 00:03:20,600
[Diagram: the key on its circle. The string snips. The key flies off in a straight line.]

110
00:03:17,550 --> 00:03:20,615
MR. ITO: Cut the string, and it flies away.

111
00:03:20,985 --> 00:03:24,135
[Earth on an invisible string around a black disc. It keeps curving round.]

112
00:03:21,085 --> 00:03:24,900
MR. ITO: We have not flown away. We are still going round.

113
00:03:25,270 --> 00:03:28,420
[Ito, quietly.]

114
00:03:25,370 --> 00:03:27,810
MR. ITO: The Sun is still there.

115
00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:31,730
[Mira and you, frozen.]

116
00:03:28,680 --> 00:03:31,195
MR. ITO: Something is in front of it.

117
00:03:32,065 --> 00:03:34,315
[A glimpse: the edge of something vast and dark, a curved wall of plates, catching the faintest red light.]

118
00:03:34,465 --> 00:03:37,615
[Ito on a ham radio, calling observatories around the world.]

119
00:03:34,565 --> 00:03:40,405
NARRATOR: Ito radios every observatory he knows. By night, the whole world has heard.

120
00:03:40,775 --> 00:03:43,925
[Day 3. Back on the roof in coats. Breath smoking.]

121
00:03:40,875 --> 00:03:45,660
NARRATOR: Day three. Ito says one last thing is still on its way from the Sun.

122
00:03:46,030 --> 00:03:48,190
[Ito points at the northern sky.]

123
00:03:46,130 --> 00:03:47,970
MR. ITO: Look! Up there!

124
00:03:48,340 --> 00:03:51,490
[The whole sky ignites: green and violet curtains over the frozen city.]

125
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:53,225
NARRATOR: A storm of particles that left the Sun four days ago. Slower than light.

126
00:03:53,595 --> 00:03:56,700
[Wide: tiny figures on the roof under the vast, rippling aurora.]

127
00:03:53,695 --> 00:03:56,480
NARRATOR: The last letter the Sun will ever send.

128
00:03:56,850 --> 00:03:59,485
[Mira, face lit green, watching.]

129
00:03:56,950 --> 00:03:58,665
MIRA: It is saying goodbye.

130
00:03:59,635 --> 00:04:02,685
[On the frosted window, a small finger draws a sun. Through it, the aurora.]

131
00:04:02,835 --> 00:04:05,985
[Day 5. The harbour. Torchlight on boats locked in new ice.]

132
00:04:02,935 --> 00:04:05,770
NARRATOR: Day five. The sea starts to freeze.

133
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:09,290
[Cut-away of the ocean: a skin of ice forms on top.]

134
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:09,330
NARRATOR: But only on top. The ice is a lid.

135
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:12,850
[The camera sinks below the ice: kilometres of dark liquid water.]

136
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:13,010
NARRATOR: Underneath, the ocean stays liquid for years.

137
00:04:13,380 --> 00:04:15,230
[Mira kneels on the ice. Under it, a fish glides past her torch beam.]

138
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:14,970
MIRA: The fish are fine!

139
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:18,530
[Earth from space, night everywhere, city lights glowing. Ice creeping out from the poles.]

140
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:20,640
NARRATOR: Day seven. Minus eighteen. Every heater on Earth is switched on at once.

141
00:04:21,010 --> 00:04:23,190
[Your city from above. Districts go dark one after another, rolling toward your street.]

142
00:04:21,110 --> 00:04:22,970
NARRATOR: The grid cannot take it.

143
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:26,095
[Your building goes dark. You strike a match and light the candles.]

144
00:04:23,440 --> 00:04:25,875
NARRATOR: Your building goes dark at midnight.

145
00:04:26,245 --> 00:04:29,055
[Torch beam in the stairwell. Grey smoke curls out from under a neighbour’s door.]

146
00:04:26,345 --> 00:04:28,835
NARRATOR: An hour later, you smell smoke.

147
00:04:29,205 --> 00:04:32,355
[The torch finds door 3B: orange firelight flickering underneath, smoke pouring out.]

148
00:04:29,305 --> 00:04:33,070
NARRATOR: Next door, someone is burning their furniture. Indoors.

149
00:04:33,440 --> 00:04:36,590
[You cover your mouth.]

150
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:37,350
NARRATOR: The smoke you can see. The carbon monoxide, you cannot.

151
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:39,805
[Running down the stairs: you with the torch, Mira at your heels, Ito with his cane, Biscuit ahead.]

152
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:39,585
NARRATOR: You get everyone out.

153
00:04:39,955 --> 00:04:42,485
[The street door bangs open into a wall of wind and snow. Biscuit bolts into the dark.]

154
00:04:40,055 --> 00:04:42,265
NARRATOR: At the door, Biscuit panics.

155
00:04:42,635 --> 00:04:45,090
[Mira screams his name into the storm. Nothing comes back.]

156
00:04:42,735 --> 00:04:43,870
MIRA: BISCUIT!

157
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:47,275
[Day 8. You walk the frozen street alone with a torch. Buried cars. Silent snow.]

158
00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:47,055
NARRATOR: You search all night.

159
00:04:47,425 --> 00:04:50,335
[You turn on the spot, sweeping the torch. Nothing.]

160
00:04:47,525 --> 00:04:50,115
NARRATOR: Then again, it is always night now.

161
00:04:50,485 --> 00:04:52,535
[Somewhere far off, a single bark (a little "WOOF!" pops out of the dark). Your head snaps round.]

162
00:04:52,685 --> 00:04:55,090
[The torch beam finds a line of fresh paw prints in the snow.]

163
00:04:52,785 --> 00:04:54,870
NARRATOR: Paw prints. Fresh ones.

164
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:58,390
[The prints lead across the street to a cloud of steam rising from a metro entrance.]

165
00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:58,350
NARRATOR: They lead to the only warm thing on the street.

166
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:01,870
[Biscuit is curled against the warm grate, frost on his whiskers. He sees you. His tail explodes.]

167
00:04:58,820 --> 00:05:02,380
NARRATOR: The metro. Warm air, rising from the tunnels.

168
00:05:02,750 --> 00:05:05,900
[Cut-away of the ground: frozen surface, then the metro tunnels, then warm rock below.]

169
00:05:02,850 --> 00:05:06,885
NARRATOR: A few metres down, the ground is still around ten degrees.

170
00:05:07,255 --> 00:05:10,090
[The warm layer glows: last summer’s heat, stored in the rock.]

171
00:05:07,355 --> 00:05:09,870
NARRATOR: It is still holding on to last summer.

172
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:13,390
[Biscuit trots toward the stairs and looks back at you. Come on.]

173
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:13,875
NARRATOR: Biscuit did not run away. He found the way down.

174
00:05:14,245 --> 00:05:17,395
[Down the metro stairs: you carrying Mira on your back, Ito, and behind them a line of torches.]

175
00:05:14,345 --> 00:05:18,005
NARRATOR: By morning, half the city has followed him underground.

176
00:05:18,375 --> 00:05:21,280
[A flash-forward, drained of colour: Ito on the frozen surface, aiming a camera at the empty sky.]

177
00:05:18,475 --> 00:05:20,760
NARRATOR: Day forty changes everything.

178
00:05:21,430 --> 00:05:24,580
[Day 30. The metro is a town: tents on the platforms, string lights, laundry lines.]

179
00:05:21,530 --> 00:05:24,620
NARRATOR: Day thirty. The metro is a city now.

180
00:05:24,990 --> 00:05:28,140
[A disused tunnel full of grow lights. Lettuce in a pink-purple glow.]

181
00:05:25,090 --> 00:05:30,750
NARRATOR: Lettuce grows under lamps in the old tunnels. Everyone is already sick of lettuce.

182
00:05:31,120 --> 00:05:34,270
[Seven a.m. The big lamps in the station roof warm up from orange to gold.]

183
00:05:31,220 --> 00:05:34,780
NARRATOR: Every morning at seven, they switch on the big lamps.

184
00:05:35,150 --> 00:05:37,385
[Faces turned up into the gold light. Mira jumps.]

185
00:05:35,250 --> 00:05:37,165
NARRATOR: People call it sunrise.

186
00:05:37,535 --> 00:05:40,685
[In a parked train carriage turned classroom, Mira stands at a chalkboard teaching younger kids to draw a sun.]

187
00:05:37,635 --> 00:05:40,970
NARRATOR: Mira teaches the little ones how to draw the Sun.

188
00:05:41,340 --> 00:05:44,490
[A small kid holds up her drawing: a grey sun. She has forgotten what colour it was.]

189
00:05:41,440 --> 00:05:44,725
NARRATOR: Some of them have already forgotten what colour it was.

190
00:05:45,695 --> 00:05:48,845
[Biscuit, noticeably rounder, receives snacks from a ring of adoring kids.]

191
00:05:45,795 --> 00:05:49,835
NARRATOR: Biscuit, meanwhile, has a fan club. And a bigger belly.

192
00:05:50,205 --> 00:05:52,340
[In the tent at night. Mira, small voice.]

193
00:05:50,305 --> 00:05:52,120
MIRA: Are we going to run out of air?

194
00:05:52,490 --> 00:05:55,640
[Diagram: Earth wrapped in its blue atmosphere. A gauge marked OXYGEN barely moves.]

195
00:05:52,590 --> 00:05:58,325
NARRATOR: No. Even with no plants making more, the air we have would last thousands of years.

196
00:05:58,695 --> 00:06:01,845
[Ito at a workbench, a firefighter’s thermal camera in pieces under a magnifier light.]

197
00:05:58,795 --> 00:06:03,035
NARRATOR: Ito spends the month rebuilding a firefighter’s heat camera.

198
00:06:03,405 --> 00:06:06,555
[He looks up at you over his glasses.]

199
00:06:03,505 --> 00:06:08,395
MR. ITO: If something is out there, it has to lose heat. Everything does.

200
00:06:08,765 --> 00:06:11,915
[Day 40. You and Ito climb out into a dead white city.]

201
00:06:08,865 --> 00:06:12,375
NARRATOR: Day forty. Noon. You climb out with Ito.

202
00:06:12,745 --> 00:06:15,830
[He raises the camera at an empty patch of black sky.]

203
00:06:12,845 --> 00:06:15,610
NARRATOR: He aims at the spot where the Sun should be.

204
00:06:15,980 --> 00:06:18,315
[The camera screen, false colour. Black sky, and in the middle, a faint warm disc.]

205
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:17,595
MR. ITO: It's warm.

206
00:06:18,465 --> 00:06:21,615
[Ito lowers the camera. His glasses have frosted.]

207
00:06:18,565 --> 00:06:22,355
MR. ITO: And it is bigger than the Sun. Twice as wide.

208
00:06:22,725 --> 00:06:25,155
[Pull back from the Sun’s position: a colossal sphere of dark plates wrapped around the star, faintly glowing at the seams.]

209
00:06:22,825 --> 00:06:24,935
NARRATOR: Not a cloud. Not a moon.

210
00:06:25,305 --> 00:06:28,455
[The camera glides around the shell. Each of its slats is wider than Earth.]

211
00:06:25,405 --> 00:06:27,745
NARRATOR: A shell. Built around a star.

212
00:06:28,915 --> 00:06:32,065
[Ito in his workshop, beside his old framed eclipse photograph, shaking his head.]

213
00:06:29,015 --> 00:06:32,155
MR. ITO: Nobody could build that in a day.

214
00:06:32,525 --> 00:06:35,675
[A window blind with its slats open, sunlight streaming through. Beside it, the Sun in a ring of the same slats, edge-on, light pouring between them.]

215
00:06:32,625 --> 00:06:37,190
MR. ITO: It was always there. Edge-on, like the slats of a blind.

216
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:40,710
[Every slat turns at once. The window goes dark. The Sun goes dark.]

217
00:06:37,660 --> 00:06:40,745
NARRATOR: And at six forty-four, someone closed them.

218
00:06:41,615 --> 00:06:44,020
[Back below, Mira looks up at you.]

219
00:06:41,715 --> 00:06:43,000
MIRA: Who built it?

220
00:06:44,170 --> 00:06:47,320
[Day 100. The lamps flicker, buzz, and die. Darkness, then a hundred torches clicking on.]

221
00:06:44,270 --> 00:06:47,355
NARRATOR: Day one hundred. The power plant fails.

222
00:06:47,725 --> 00:06:50,875
[Ito limps off into the dark tunnel with the engineers, torch in hand.]

223
00:06:47,825 --> 00:06:51,540
NARRATOR: Ito goes with the engineers. He is seventy-four.

224
00:06:51,910 --> 00:06:54,045
[Under every blanket you own, you and Mira shiver. Breath smoking even down here.]

225
00:06:52,010 --> 00:06:53,825
NARRATOR: The tunnels get cold.

226
00:06:54,195 --> 00:06:57,345
[Your face in the last candle light.]

227
00:06:54,295 --> 00:06:57,855
NARRATOR: For the first time, you wonder if this is how it ends.

228
00:06:59,125 --> 00:07:02,160
[Day 180. The lamps blaze back on. The whole station roars.]

229
00:06:59,225 --> 00:07:01,940
NARRATOR: Day one eighty. The lights come back.

230
00:07:02,310 --> 00:07:05,460
[Ito walks back out of the tunnel, black with grease, grinning. Mira runs into him.]

231
00:07:02,410 --> 00:07:06,845
NARRATOR: Ito comes back covered in grease and very pleased with himself.

232
00:07:07,215 --> 00:07:10,365
[The radio again, crackling.]

233
00:07:07,315 --> 00:07:13,380
(RADIO): Submarine crews report the life around deep sea vents is completely unaffected.

234
00:07:13,750 --> 00:07:16,900
[The sea floor: a black smoker, tube worms, crabs, ghostly shrimp. Thriving.]

235
00:07:13,850 --> 00:07:17,440
NARRATOR: Down there, life runs on heat from inside the Earth.

236
00:07:17,810 --> 00:07:20,165
[Close on a crab waving a claw in the warm shimmer.]

237
00:07:17,910 --> 00:07:19,945
NARRATOR: It never needed the Sun.

238
00:07:20,315 --> 00:07:22,625
[Ito, smiling for the first time in months.]

239
00:07:20,415 --> 00:07:22,405
MR. ITO: Neither will we.

240
00:07:22,775 --> 00:07:25,925
[Day 300. The surface again. Snow squeaks underfoot like polystyrene. Ito’s camera has a bigger lens.]

241
00:07:22,875 --> 00:07:26,665
NARRATOR: Day three hundred. Ito's camera is better now.

242
00:07:27,035 --> 00:07:30,185
[Thermal view: the warm disc, and from it a thin glowing thread running off into space.]

243
00:07:27,135 --> 00:07:32,070
NARRATOR: A thin, warm thread is leaving the shell. Dust, glowing in its path.

244
00:07:32,440 --> 00:07:35,325
[Map of the solar system: the shell, and a straight line leaving it, pointing away from every planet.]

245
00:07:32,540 --> 00:07:35,105
MR. ITO: It is not blocking the Sun.

246
00:07:35,475 --> 00:07:38,460
[Ito, barely a whisper.]

247
00:07:35,575 --> 00:07:37,440
MR. ITO: It is taking it.

248
00:07:38,610 --> 00:07:41,760
[The shell from far away, a needle of energy leaving it toward a faint, distant star.]

249
00:07:38,710 --> 00:07:41,445
NARRATOR: All of the Sun’s light, sent somewhere else.

250
00:07:42,415 --> 00:07:45,565
[Day 365. All of you on the surface, tiny under the brightest Milky Way anyone has ever seen.]

251
00:07:42,515 --> 00:07:46,050
NARRATOR: Day three sixty-five. Minus seventy-three.

252
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:49,225
[The Milky Way, blazing from horizon to horizon over the frozen skyline.]

253
00:07:46,520 --> 00:07:49,005
NARRATOR: The clearest sky in human history.

254
00:07:49,375 --> 00:07:52,525
[Mira grins up at you.]

255
00:07:49,475 --> 00:07:52,989
MIRA: One whole lap around the Sun. Happy birthday, Earth.

256
00:07:53,359 --> 00:07:55,809
[In space: one slat of the shell turns back, for one second. A blade of sunlight escapes.]

257
00:07:55,959 --> 00:07:59,109
[Eight minutes later: for one second the whole frozen world turns gold. Then black.]

258
00:07:56,059 --> 00:07:59,994
NARRATOR: Eight minutes later, the Sun comes back. For one second.

259
00:08:00,964 --> 00:08:03,614
[Mira, eyes shining. She saw it. A real one.]

260
00:08:03,764 --> 00:08:06,914
[Below, every radio in the station hisses at once. A rhythm of pulses.]

261
00:08:03,864 --> 00:08:07,024
NARRATOR: Then every radio on Earth picks up a signal.

262
00:08:07,394 --> 00:08:10,399
[Space: rings of radio waves pulse outward from the shell, washing over the planets.]

263
00:08:07,494 --> 00:08:10,179
NARRATOR: It is coming from the direction of the Sun.

264
00:08:10,549 --> 00:08:13,699
[Ito listens to the bursts of pulses and counts them: one, then two, then three.]

265
00:08:10,649 --> 00:08:11,689
MR. ITO: One.

266
00:08:12,539 --> 00:08:13,579
MR. ITO: Two.

267
00:08:14,429 --> 00:08:15,569
MR. ITO: Three.

268
00:08:16,739 --> 00:08:19,624
[The planets in a row. Mercury lights up. Venus. Earth.]

269
00:08:16,839 --> 00:08:19,404
MR. ITO: It is counting the planets.

270
00:08:19,774 --> 00:08:22,834
[The count stops on Earth. Earth, alone, highlighted.]

271
00:08:19,874 --> 00:08:21,614
NARRATOR: And it stopped at three.

272
00:08:22,984 --> 00:08:25,712
[Mira, very quietly, looks at you.]

273
00:08:23,084 --> 00:08:24,292
MIRA: That's us.

274
00:08:25,862 --> 00:08:29,012
[The end screen: the shell turning, its three pulses, PART 2 · THE REPLY, and room for the subscribe and next-video cards.]

275
00:08:25,962 --> 00:08:28,947
NARRATOR: What would you say back? Tell us in the comments.

276
00:08:29,297 --> 00:08:33,737
NARRATOR: Part two is coming. Subscribe, so you're here when it replies.
